const routeAnalysis: Record<string, string> = {
  'hr-new-hire-start-date-evidence-reconciliation': 'For a recurring HR support queue, the practical review begins by labeling every date before comparing values. A target date belongs to planning; an accepted offer or authorized hiring record belongs to the employment decision; an HRIS entry belongs to data administration; and a payroll acknowledgment belongs to downstream processing. The coordinator should quote the field label and source, not paraphrase a confusing screen. If two sources disagree, the packet should identify the consequence of each possible date, such as a missed onboarding task or a payroll cutoff, then ask the named owner to resolve it. This is more useful than a broad escalation that says only “start date mismatch.” Reviewers should also check whether a future-dated change was deliberately staged and whether an older proposal was superseded. A completed task can be reopened when its governing date changes. The evidence-led operating choice is to preserve the conflict, restrict access to offer and identity detail, and make the next decision small enough for the authorized owner to answer.',
  'hr-employee-manager-change-effective-date-governance': 'The route-specific test is whether a later reviewer can distinguish a communication event from an authority event. Start with the approved change record, then compare the effective interval against the HRIS relationship, approval routing, access group, and recurring report snapshot. If one system changes early, label it as out of sequence rather than silently correcting another system. A manager change may also create a temporary delegation or a future-dated approval, so the packet should state whether the old owner remains active until the effective date. Support should record a system owner for each dependency and a checkpoint for the response. It should not send an organizational announcement merely because a field changed. When the source approval is missing, the correct status is authorization pending. When approval exists but a dependent system disagrees, the correct status is propagation exception. This distinction helps Human Resources Outsourced coordinate sensitive work without widening access or making an organizational decision by accident.',
  'hr-employee-termination-final-payroll-input-evidence': 'The most useful termination review is a sequence, not a single checklist. First confirm that an authorized owner created the event. Next identify the effective meaning and compare it with last day worked, payroll timing, benefits processing, and access instructions. Then record which owner acknowledged each downstream action. If the event is withdrawn or changed, preserve the original and connect the superseding decision; do not overwrite history. A support queue can say “payroll input awaiting owner confirmation” or “security instruction acknowledged” without stating a conclusion about the employment relationship. Sensitive reasons and allegations should remain in the restricted source. The review should ask whether an uncompleted handoff creates a defined operational risk and who owns it, not whether a coordinator believes the event is fair. That disciplined separation is particularly important when several systems use different clocks and when a late change is requested near a cutoff.',
  'hr-leave-request-approval-effective-date-reconciliation': 'Route-specific analysis should start with the policy version and the approval state, then trace the dates into the absence and balance records. A manager calendar entry is useful context but should not be treated as an approval unless the policy says it is authoritative. If an approved absence is missing from a balance, classify the issue as posting or timing until the owner confirms a rule problem. If a request has medical or protected-status information, the general queue should retain only a restricted reference and routing category. The coordinator can ask for a missing field through the approved channel and report the impact on a payroll or scheduling checkpoint. It should not ask the employee to repeat sensitive detail to make a tracker complete. Reviewers should preserve withdrawn, reversed, and corrected events because those states explain why a current balance differs from an earlier snapshot. A clear pending state is evidence of control, not a service failure.',
  'hr-benefits-change-event-vendor-acknowledgment': 'In a benefits queue, the key interpretive discipline is to translate each vendor response literally. Receipt means the transaction arrived; acceptance may mean it passed a file rule; processing may still be pending; and a portal display may lag all three. The packet should name which statement the response supports and which question remains for the plan owner. A coordinator can compare an approved employment event to the transaction identifier and ask a vendor about a defined error. It should not infer coverage from a successful upload or copy dependent data into a broadly visible report. If a change is duplicated, preserve both identifiers and route the owner’s correction decision. If the plan version is unclear, stop at evidence collection. This route is therefore useful as a test of truthful status language: “vendor accepted transaction” is supportable when documented, while “employee is covered” requires authority and facts outside the support lane.',
  'hr-recruiting-candidate-disposition-evidence-boundaries': 'A practical closeout review should compare the requisition stage, decision owner, approved disposition category, and communication event without opening more candidate content than necessary. If a candidate withdrew, preserve the candidate’s stated event separately from an employer disposition. If an interviewer provided free-text feedback, keep it in the restricted recruiting system and do not rewrite it into a coded reason. If the hiring owner has not decided, leave the requisition or candidate state pending. The support role can identify missing evidence, schedule a follow-up, and confirm that an approved message was sent. It cannot make a sparse record look complete by choosing a reason after the fact. Reviewers should sample status reversals and late dispositions because those cases reveal whether the system preserves history. The evidence goal is an explainable sequence from stage to authorized decision to communication, not a polished narrative about a candidate.',
  'hr-performance-review-cycle-calibration-records': 'A route-specific review should begin with the template version and assigned population, because a completed form from an older cycle may not be comparable with a current form. Then trace draft, submission, calibration, final approval, delivery, acknowledgment, and reopen events. A support coordinator can report that a review is submitted but not calibrated, or delivered but not acknowledged. Those states are more honest than a single complete flag. Keep comments and ratings in the approved restricted system, and use aggregate counts in the operations report. If an employee disputes a review, preserve the dispute and route it; do not edit the final record to remove friction. A later reviewer should see who changed the version and why. This distinction protects the review owner and prevents administration from becoming an unofficial evaluator. The evidence proves process movement and ownership; it does not prove that a rating accurately describes performance.',
  'hr-employee-hris-identity-duplicate-resolution': 'The route-specific decision gate is authority to link, not confidence in a matching algorithm. A suspected duplicate should first be assigned a protected case identifier and compared using only approved attributes. Then identify affected payroll, benefits, access, and reporting relationships before proposing any merge. A false positive can erase history or expose one worker’s data to another profile, so the correction packet should include a pre-change reference and downstream recovery plan. Support may classify a signal as likely duplicate, false positive, or disputed under a defined rule, but a confidence label is not approval. If the source identity is itself uncertain, escalate to the data owner and stop automated action. After an approved correction, reconcile each dependent system and preserve responses. The quality test is whether the owner can understand both the evidence for the link and the cost of being wrong without opening a broad collection of identity documents.',
  'hr-policy-update-effective-date-employee-acknowledgment': 'The route-specific control is a version-to-audience map. For each published policy, identify the approved file, effective date, audience snapshot, delivery destination, language derivative, and acknowledgment rule. If an audience member received an earlier version, do not count the later campaign as a correction until the policy owner approves the remedy. A bounced email, inaccessible link, or disputed translation should remain a distinct exception. Support can resend approved content and record delivery; it cannot alter the policy to resolve a question or mark an acknowledgment on the employee’s behalf. Reporting should say “acknowledged version 3” rather than merely “acknowledged.” That wording preserves the evidence needed when a policy is superseded. The result is an honest communication record: it shows what the organization approved and sent, while leaving interpretation, employee questions, and legal sufficiency to the responsible owner.',
  'hr-employee-training-assignment-due-date-integrity': 'For this route, the most revealing review compares the assigned population at the start of the cycle with the population used in the completion report. New hires, transfers, exemptions, and course-version changes should be shown as movements, not hidden denominator edits. A learner who opened a course but did not satisfy the defined completion rule remains incomplete or in progress according to the approved model. A certificate does not establish competence unless the owner has defined that evidence. Support can reconcile the learning system, send approved reminders, and identify overdue or disputed records. It should not change a due date because a manager asks informally or label a learner competent. Reviewers should preserve late completions and corrected records so the final percentage can be understood later. The evidence-led operating choice is to report states and scope, then route remediation to the training or safety owner.',
};
const reviewImplications: Record<string, string> = {
  'hr-new-hire-start-date-evidence-reconciliation': 'A reviewer can test this route by asking five bounded questions: which source approved the date, what does each competing timestamp mean, which downstream owner acknowledged the handoff, what consequence follows if the date changes, and what decision remains open? The answers should be drawn from linked records, not a coordinator’s memory. If the date is changed, retain the prior proposal and record the authority and effective reason. If no owner has resolved the conflict, the report should say pending and identify the next checkpoint. This makes the article relevant to recurring Human Resources Outsourced work: the value is not a faster edit but a clearer decision packet. The company’s support lane can reduce administrative friction by finding the mismatch early, limiting data exposure, and ensuring the right owner receives a concise question. It cannot remove the underlying employment judgment. The evidence supports that boundary because a date has operational consequences beyond the field where it appears.',
  'hr-employee-manager-change-effective-date-governance': 'A reviewer should inspect one ordinary change, one future-dated change, and one reversal. For each, compare the approved event with the relationship field, approval route, access consequence, and report snapshot. The review should record whether a mismatch is expected sequencing, missing approval, stale propagation, or an unauthorized change. That classification is more informative than a generic error count and gives the system owner a specific next action. It also prevents a support team from becoming the hidden author of an organizational chart. The company’s recurring HR administration work benefits when the same evidence pattern can be reused for a manager change while the substantive content stays with the client owner. A clean handoff names the old and new state, the effective interval, and the system still out of sequence. It should not expose a broad employee population merely to prove one relationship. Access and reporting effects are separate review questions even when they originate in one approved decision.',
  'hr-employee-termination-final-payroll-input-evidence': 'A useful quality review samples a termination near a payroll cutoff, a future-dated event, a withdrawn event, and an event with an unresolved downstream acknowledgment. It asks whether the source approval, date meaning, worker reference, and owner decisions are linked without duplicating sensitive narrative. The report should separate a delayed acknowledgment from a disputed employment fact. That distinction lets Human Resources Outsourced support escalate the right issue to payroll, benefits, security, HR, or legal without implying a conclusion. It also creates honest status language for a client’s internal queue: prepared, held, accepted, or owner review are different states. The administrative lane succeeds when it prevents a missing decision from becoming an accidental system change. A reviewer should be able to reconstruct what happened after the fact and see who had authority at each stage. If that chain is absent, the proper remedy is evidence collection and escalation, not a guessed date or an overwritten history.',
  'hr-leave-request-approval-effective-date-reconciliation': 'Reviewers should compare a routine approved request, a changed date range, a reversed absence, and a request that contains a sensitive escalation signal. The objective is to see whether the event ledger preserves policy version, approval owner, effective dates, and balance snapshot while keeping the narrative restricted. A coordinator can report that a balance is awaiting posting or that an approval reference is missing; it cannot convert either state into an entitlement decision. This creates a useful operating boundary for Human Resources Outsourced: the support team can reconcile defined records and make the queue legible, while the employer’s HR or leave owner handles legal and policy meaning. The report should retain a denominator for open exceptions and note the period measured. It should not use closure speed as a proxy for fairness or compliance. A later reviewer needs enough evidence to explain the balance difference without receiving unrelated health, family, or employment details.',
  'hr-benefits-change-event-vendor-acknowledgment': 'A reviewer should take one accepted transaction, one rejected transaction, one stale display, and one duplicate submission and write the exact proposition each response supports. The accepted case may support receipt or processing, but only the plan owner can connect that event to eligibility and coverage. This simple exercise exposes overclaiming in benefits queues. It also gives Human Resources Outsourced a practical support boundary: reconcile identifiers, preserve vendor messages, protect participant data, and route the unresolved plan question. If a vendor response is ambiguous, record the ambiguity and ask a bounded question rather than forwarding a full record to a wider audience. Measures should show how many transactions have an owner decision, not just how many files were delivered. The record should preserve the plan or policy version that governed the event, because a later rule may not explain an earlier transaction. Evidence quality is therefore both chronological and contextual.',
  'hr-recruiting-candidate-disposition-evidence-boundaries': 'A reviewer should sample a withdrawal, a non-selection, a reopened requisition, a changed disposition, and a candidate whose communication was delayed. For each, ask whether the status came from an authorized decision, whether the communication event is preserved, and whether the reason was copied or invented. This is a meaningful quality test for Human Resources Outsourced coordination because scheduling and record maintenance can be accurate without giving support authority over selection. The report should show incomplete ownership rather than fill it with a generic rejection code. It should also keep candidate narratives in the system designed for them, using a narrow reference in operational reporting. The result is a more defensible record of process movement and less unnecessary exposure of personal information. A later reviewer can understand why the status changed without assuming that the coordinator judged the candidate. Facts about events and decisions remain distinct from analysis about process controls.',
  'hr-performance-review-cycle-calibration-records': 'The most revealing sample includes a review submitted before calibration, a review changed during calibration, an employee acknowledgment without agreement, and a reopened review. The reviewer should verify version, owner, timestamps, and change reason, while avoiding unnecessary access to comments. This keeps the support lane focused on process evidence. Human Resources Outsourced can report where a cycle is waiting, delivered, or reopened, but the manager and HR owner decide performance meaning. A dashboard should therefore show separate counts for submission, approval, delivery, acknowledgment, and exception. It should not compress them into a percentage that implies quality or employee acceptance. The method also protects later review: a changed rating is explainable when the approved version and calibration event are linked. A missing link is a control gap, not an invitation for administration to rewrite the record. Process evidence is valuable precisely because it leaves substantive judgment with its owner.',
  'hr-employee-hris-identity-duplicate-resolution': 'A reviewer should test a likely duplicate, a false positive, a former-worker relationship, and a case with conflicting source attributes. The packet should show what comparison was authorized, which evidence was considered, who decided, and how payroll, benefits, access, and reporting consequences were checked. It should not include a full identity dossier when a protected reference is enough. Human Resources Outsourced support can make the question smaller and safer by separating detection from correction. The decision owner needs both the evidence for a link and the consequence of a mistaken merge. A count of merges is not a quality measure by itself; false positives, unresolved age, and downstream repair status provide more useful context. After a correction, retain the pre-change reference and system responses. If authority or identity remains disputed, the appropriate state is owner review. That honest pause protects history and prevents a convenience match from becoming a permanent data decision.',
  'hr-policy-update-effective-date-employee-acknowledgment': 'A reviewer should compare a policy that was approved late, a translated derivative, a bounced delivery, and an acknowledgment recorded after supersession. The test is whether the register identifies the version and effective date that the acknowledgment actually concerns. Human Resources Outsourced support can publish approved material, monitor delivery, and report exceptions, but it should not interpret a policy question or change wording to increase completion. The report should preserve audience changes and distinguish delivery from acknowledgment. If an employee received an obsolete version, that is a version-control exception even if a later campaign reaches the same person. This makes the evidence useful to the policy owner without overstating legal or behavioral conclusions. A record that says “acknowledged version 3” is stronger than “complete,” because it allows a later reviewer to inspect the governing document and communication event. The same discipline protects translated and accessible versions from being treated as interchangeable files.',
  'hr-employee-training-assignment-due-date-integrity': 'A reviewer should compare a stable course cycle with a cycle that includes transfers, exemptions, a revised course version, and late completions. It should be possible to explain the assigned denominator, the due-date rule, every state change, and the owner’s treatment of exceptions. Human Resources Outsourced support can reconcile records and send approved reminders, but it cannot decide that a learner is competent or waive a requirement. A completion report should therefore include scope and version next to the percentage. If an assessment is sensitive, keep its detail restricted and report only the defined state. The evidence-led value is better decision support, not a stronger claim than the system permits. A lower but transparent completion figure may be more useful than a high figure produced by excluding overdue or transferred workers. This keeps training administration factual and gives the qualified owner a clear basis for remediation.',
};
